    {
      "paper_id": "doi:10.1016/j.euroecorev.2026.105432",
      "sequence": 11,
      "source_sequence": 11,
      "article_type": "research-article",
      "title_en": "Fickle fossils: Economic growth, coal, and the European oil invasion, 1900–2015",
      "title_cn": "变化无常的化石：经济增长、煤炭和欧洲石油入侵，1900-2015",
      "authors": [
        "Miriam Fritzsche",
        "Nikolaus Wolf"
      ],
      "abstract_en": "Fossil fuels have shaped the European economy since the industrial revolution. We use new long-run panel data to analyse the effect of both, coal and oil on economic growth between 1900 and 2015, exploiting variation at the level of European NUTS2 and NUTS3 regions. We show that the reversal of fortune of coal regions resulted from the second energy transition. Specifically, an “oil invasion” in the early 1960s turned regional coal abundance from a blessing into a curse. Human capital accumulation contributed to this reversal of fortune and fully explains the negative effects until today. Moreover, we find substantial heterogeneity between former coal regions that is in line with Glaeser’s “reinvention hypothesis”: regions with a higher skill-level adjusted much better to the decline of coal. In particular, we show that coal regions with a higher urban density before 1800 were much more resilient than others.",
      "abstract_cn": "自工业革命以来，化石燃料塑造了欧洲经济。我们使用新的长期面板数据来分析1900和2015之间煤炭和石油对经济增长的影响，利用欧洲 NUTS2 和 NUTS3 地区水平的变化。我们表明，煤炭地区命运的逆转是第二能源转型的结果。具体来说，1960早期的“石油入侵”使该地区煤炭丰富从祝福变成了诅咒。人力资本积累促成了这种命运的逆转，并充分解释了迄今为止的负面影响。此外，我们发现前煤炭地区之间存在显着的异质性，这符合格莱泽的“重塑假说”：技能水平较高的地区能够更好地适应煤炭的衰退。特别是，我们表明1800之前城市密度较高的煤炭地区比其他地区更具弹性。",
      "abstract_snippet_en": "",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.euroecorev.2026.105432",
